"491">tableLayout: "fixed"</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"492" data-end=3D"509">
<p data-start=3D"494" data-end=3D"509"><code data-start=3D"494" data-end=3D=
"509">width: "100%"</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"511" data-end=3D"566">After removal, your table format wil=
l return to normal.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"568" data-end=3D"571">
<h1 data-start=3D"573" data-end=3D"587">=E2=9D=97Important</h1>
<p data-start=3D"588" data-end=3D"772">The reason the layout looked =E2=80=
=9Cseriously upset=E2=80=9D is because <code data-start=3D"646" data-end=3D=
"667">table-layout: fixed</code> forces <strong data-start=3D"675" data-end=
=3D"705">all columns to equal width</strong>, which breaks tables with many=
 small and large columns like yours.</p>
<p data-start=3D"774" data-end=3D"812">We will NOT use that approach anymor=
e.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"814" data-end=3D"817">
<h1 data-start=3D"819" data-end=3D"874">=E2=AD=90 Now let=E2=80=99s fix =E2=
=80=9COther Requirement=E2=80=9D the correct way</h1>
